00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:07.909 align:start position:0%
a visit to the dentist's office is rife  with sensations sounds and sights  some may be terrifying  while others are meant to relax you  sounds like these might also reduce pain  studies from as early as the 1960s have  shown that sounds including music can  alleviate pain in people  we know that when certain types of  sounds are around activity changes in  pain related parts of the brain  but exactly how sound turns into pain  relief is still uncertain  a new study took to the simpler smaller  brain of mice to understand how music  can make the pain go away  first the researchers inflamed the  mice's paws  they then played different sounds  classical music  a dissonant version of the same song  and white noise  from behavioral tests they found that  all three of these kinds of sounds  provided pain relief  but only at a certain volume five  decibels above the ambient noise of the  room  or about the sound of a conversation  with someone  any louder and the effect was gone  after exposing the mice to this subtle  white noise for multiple days  the pain relief continued  even a day or two after the exposure to  the white noise was stopped  if the sounds were merely distractions  from the pain then lasting pain relief  would not be expected  after recording the mice's brain  activity while listening to this ideal  level of noise the team found that fewer  neurons fired in the mice's auditory  cortex the hub of sound processing  the signal from the auditory cortex  connected to the thalamus  which processes every sense except smell  hearing the sounds reduce the auditory  inputs to this key pain processing  region  seemingly relieving pain in mice  in humans more areas are involved in  pain processing so the pathway is likely  more complex  and we might be more sensitive to noise  type  classical music or jazz might be the  best pain relievers for us  but this study provides clues as to what  might be happening in mouse brains and  beyond  and for now could be used to make lab  mice a little more comfortable during  experiments  until we uncover the mechanism in people  it won't hurt us to keep listening to  music in the dentist's chair  [Music]  you
 

